# Chapter 29 — Listening

He spent most of the night looking for the edges of it, and found that it had none.

He lay on his back in the dark with the blanket up to his chin and the frost growing on the window, and turned his attention outward, onto his own skin, where the new thing lay. He could feel that it was there. That was the strange part: he could feel it the way you feel a coat you have been wearing all day, not as weight exactly, only as the knowledge that something is on you. It lay over his arms and his back and his face, very thin, and it did not move, and it did not answer.

He tried asking it the way he asked the Wind. A drop, sharp, all at once, the way the hip went. Nothing dropped, because there was nowhere in the coat to drop from. He tried the way of the giving face: found a beat in his breathing, slow, and let it run, and waited for the new thing to come up and ride on it. Nothing rode. He tried simply wanting it, hard, with his jaw set, which had never worked on anything in his life and did not work now. Every time he leaned on it his attention slid off, like a hand on a frosted window, and he was lying in a cold room in the dark with his eyes open, being a fool.

Toward morning he stopped, and lay there with his hands on the blanket, and thought about the Wind.

He had forgotten how long it had taken; that was the thing he made himself remember in the dark. It had been his for so long now that he had forgotten how useless it had been at the start. It had come at Torvin's and then done nothing he asked for weeks, and it had come by itself on the worst mornings and never on the good ones. Lira had stood over him in the cold with a stick all that winter calling *stop* at every burst, and he had fallen on the straw more times than he had landed.

It had been a whole season before he could ask it for anything and be sure of an answer. The giving face had been the same, in its way: two misfires up the wrist before it would ride a strike on the beat. Twice already he had started from nothing like this. He had only forgotten what nothing felt like.

He slept for an hour after that, and dreamed nothing he remembered.

Brom was in the alcove before him, for once. He had the lamp lit and his coat on its nail and his hands not wound, and he was standing in the middle of the stone with his arms hanging and his weight back, looking at nothing. When Cael came in at the arch he did not move.

"No crosses today," he said. "Nobody's hitting anybody. You're going to stand there, or sit, whatever you like, and try to find me with it. I'm going to make it easy." He did something then that Cael could not see, and did not need to see. "I'm as loud as I go. I'm standing here with the read open all the way and nothing else in my head. If you can't hear me now, you won't hear anybody."

"And you'll watch."

"I'll watch you not be able to." Brom said it without unkindness, as a plain forecast, like weather. "Then I'll tell you why. I've had two years with mine. I'm the only person in this city who knows what it's meant to feel like from the inside, and I'll be honest with you: I don't know how to say it. Nobody ever had to say it to me." He moved his shoulders. "So I'll watch, and when I see what you're doing wrong I'll try to find a word for it."

Cael stood three paces off on the cold stone and began.

The first hour was nothing. He did everything he had done in the night, and some things he had not thought of: standing still, sitting on the bench, sitting on the floor, his eyes open, his eyes shut, his breath held, his breath run out slow to the bottom. He tried looking straight at Brom and he tried looking away. He tried the gaze, the full depth of it, all six hooks down into the big man's body, in case the new thing would come up on the back of the old one, and got an ache behind his forehead from it and nothing else at all.

Three paces away a man stood with the read open as far as it went, and Cael, who had a piece of that same read lying on every inch of his skin, could not feel him any more than he could feel the moon.

By the end of the hour the not-feeling had a shape of its own. It was like standing in an empty yard calling a dog by its name, knowing the dog was somewhere close, and that it was his, and that it was not going to come.

---

"You're commanding it," said Brom.

Cael opened his eyes.

Brom had sat down on the bench at some point in the last quarter-hour without Cael's noticing, and he was leaning forward with his forearms on his knees and his big hands loose between them, and he was frowning, not at Cael, but at something just in front of himself, as if the word he wanted were hanging in the air there and he had nearly got hold of it.

"Every time," said Brom. "I can see you do it. You go still, and for a moment I think you've got it, and then I watch you get ready, the way you get ready on the floor before you go. It's the same every time. Your shoulders come down. Your eyes fix. Your breath goes short. You get organised, like a man about to throw something." He opened one hand. "You're aiming. It doesn't aim."

"Then what does it do?"

Brom did not answer at once. He looked at his own open hand, and Cael saw him go somewhere, a long way off, further than the river city.

"I said at the wall I'd tell you the rest of the estate wall," he said. "The yard man. The month of sitting." He turned the hand over. "I thought he'd put me there to be out of the way. While they talked about me in the long room. I hated him for it. Every morning for a month, after the station, he sat me down on the flags with my back to the wall of the kitchen garden and said *stay there till I call you*, and went in, and left me. No post. No falls. Nothing. Just the wall, and the cold, and the gardeners going by with their barrows. I was fourteen and I'd just been read Copper and everybody in the house had gone quiet, and I sat there with nothing to do but be angry."

"And then?"

"And then about the third week I stopped being angry, because you can't keep it up that long with nothing to feed it. I stopped listening for him to call me. I stopped listening for anything." Brom was quiet a moment. "That was the morning I first felt one of the gardeners on the other side of the wall. Through the brick. An old man with a barrow, and I could feel him going along, his weight and his tiredness and the barrow, like a man upstairs. I didn't know what it was. I nearly got up and ran."

"He was teaching you."

"He was teaching me. The old way. The way the houses do it, I found out after, in a book in the river city that cost me a week's purse to read." Brom closed the hand. "He was the only one in that whole house who did a single thing about what I could actually do. He never said so. He let me hate him for a month and never told me." He looked up at Cael. "I didn't work it out till the boat shed. Two years too late to thank him."

Cael sat with that.

He was thinking, for no reason he could at first find, of the mill at home, and the morning by the wheel-house when Hesk had put his small hand flat on the great wooden shaft and said *now watch the water*. He had stood there with his palm on the wood, doing nothing, waiting, and felt nothing for so long he thought he was being teased. And then the knock had come up through the shaft into his hand, and the whole slow strength of the river with it. Hesk had not told him to feel for it. Hesk had told him to watch the water and keep his hand where it was.

*Like a hand on a beam*, Brom had said, in the alcove, about the read. *You feel who's moving on the floor above.*

It was the same hand: a boy's hand on a shaft and a man's hand on a beam and a boy of fourteen with his back against a garden wall. None of them were reaching; they were all only resting there, flat, on something that carried weight, and letting the weight come.

The alcove was quiet, and out on the floor the sweepers had begun, the long sound of the brooms.

"You can't listen and talk at once," said Brom. "That's the whole of it, I think. That's what the wall was for. And everything you've got is talking. The step's talking. The thing in your arm's talking. The way you watch people, the hooks, that's the loudest of all; it's you asking them questions as fast as they can answer." He sat back. "This doesn't ask. You don't send it anywhere. You leave the door open and sit down, and whatever's out there walks in when it likes."

---

Getting quiet enough turned out to be the hardest thing anybody had ever asked him to do.

He had been quiet all his life, on the outside. He could sit on a bench for six hours and not move. But it was a hunter's quiet, a quiet with a question in the middle of it, and the question never stopped. He sat on the alcove floor with his legs crossed and his hands on his knees and tried to stop asking, and found that his attention would not be still.

It was a trained thing, and he had spent a year training it. Asked to point at nothing and wait, it twitched and went looking, like a dog that has been taught to fetch, and every time it went looking, it was aiming again, and Brom on the bench said, quietly, "There," and Cael knew he had lost it before he had found it.

There turned out to be a great many ways to fail at doing nothing.

He failed by counting his own breath, because counting was a thing he did, and a count was a question. He failed by listening hard for Brom, which was aiming. He failed by deciding not to listen for Brom, and then listening to find out whether he was listening. He failed by beginning, without meaning to, to go down into the hooks on Brom's resting body, *dormant, dormant*, as he had for twelve days from that same bench. Once he failed by noticing that his left knee hurt, and then by noticing that he was noticing, and then by laughing at himself, which Brom said was the closest he had come all morning.

He lost count of the failures somewhere past thirty. Brom did not. Brom said, at one point, "That's forty," in the voice of a man reading the number off a slate, and Cael said something short and unkind about the number forty, and Brom's mouth went in at the corner.

After the fortieth he stopped trying. He did not decide to; there was nothing left to try with. He was tired all the way down, and his head ached from the gaze, and his knees ached from the floor, and he let his hands lie open on them and thought, for no reason, about lying on his back on the main floor a week ago with his legs gone, looking up at the lamps.

He had had nothing to do then; that was what he remembered. The bout was over and he was not getting up. There had been nothing left in the world to aim at, and so for the length of three breaths he had simply lain there and looked at the field of small flames on the low ceiling, and the dark beams running across it, and let them be there.

He let himself be there again.

And something came in.

It was not subtle, and that was what shocked him afterward, when he could think. He had expected something faint, something he would have to strain after, a whisper at the edge of things, and it was not a whisper.

Three paces in front of him, on the bench, there was a man, and Cael could feel him the way you feel a fire at your back when you are standing in a cold room with your coat to it: all at once, without turning round, exactly where it is. It pressed on the front of him. It was big, and warm, and steady, and alive, alive in a way that nothing else in the alcove was, the wall and the post and the lamp all gone dim and flat beside it.

And it had a shape. He could not have said what the shape was; he had no words yet. But it was the same shape he had felt in the first breath of the notice, the great blurred weight like a door; and it was also, he understood with a jolt that nearly lost him the whole of it, the thing that had been laid on him for a month. *This* was what Brom had been doing at the end of the east bench, at the rope, a bench's length off across the alcove. Cael was feeling the hand that had been feeling him.

He held it. One breath. Two. He knew, without any measuring, which way it lay from him, and that it was close; how close, he could not tell, the distance smeared like a lamp seen through steam. Three breaths. Four.

Brom shifted his weight on the bench.

It was a small movement, nothing, a big man settling. But Cael's attention went to it. It went the way it had been trained to go, toward the movement, quick, to find out what it meant; and the moment it went, it was aiming, and the fire at his back was gone, and he was sitting on a cold floor in a lamplit alcove with his eyes wide open and his heart going like a hammer.

---

"Well?" said Brom.

"I had you." Cael's voice came out strange. "Four breaths. You moved and I looked."

"I know. I felt you go."

Cael did not answer that, because the headache had arrived.

It came about a minute after, quite suddenly, as if it had been waiting politely at the door. It was not like the eye-ache of the gaze, which lived behind his forehead and grew slowly through a night. It was a band, and it went round his head from temple to temple behind his eyes and pulled tight, and stayed tight, and the alcove lamp became a thing he did not want to look at. He sat with his eyes shut and his palms cupped over them, keeping the lamp out.

He knew what it was: the same thing the gaze spent, the same thing that went out of him on a long night on the east bench and came back with sleep; only the gaze took a whole bout to draw that much, and this had drawn it in four breaths. Four breaths of feeling the man had cost him what an evening of watching cost. The Wind spent his hip and his breath. The giving face spent the hollow under his ribs. This spent something he could only call his attention itself, the plain stuff he did all his looking with, and it spent it the way a crack in a jug spends water.

"Mine costs me nothing," said Brom, from the bench. "Now. Two years of it coming out of me every day, and the bill got smaller every month." He came across and sat down on the floor in front of Cael, cross-legged, nearer than before; a forearm off, the sparring distance. "Again. When the head lets you. This time I'm not going to sit like a post. I'll shift about, slow. See if it stays on me."

He could not keep him, and that was the afternoon.

He tried it first with the band still round his head, because he was impatient, and got nothing at all, and Brom told him to wait. So he waited, sitting on the floor with his eyes shut, while the band loosened a notch at a time, and Brom sat in front of him and said nothing and did not seem to mind the waiting.

It occurred to Cael, sitting there, that the big man had spent most of his life waiting for other people to come to him, on his heels, on a post, on a floor, and that he was better at it than anybody Cael had ever met, Lira included. He was waiting now, for a boy with a headache to be able to feel him. It did not seem to cost him anything.

They went on for two more hours, with rests for the band to loosen, and he found the big man twice more, once for three breaths and once for five. Each time Brom moved, slowly, a hand lifted off a knee, a shift of weight, and each time Cael's attention went after the movement and the channel shut. It survived only when nothing moved, Brom or Cael either. When Cael himself moved, even a little, even only turning his head to rest his neck, it went before he had finished turning.

And it would not share.

He found that out by trying, near the end, when he was tired and stupid and the band had settled into a steady ache. He found Brom, and held him, a breath, two, and then very carefully, with the smallest piece of himself he could spare, he let the hip lean, the way it leaned before a burst, only the first beginning of the Wind.

The listening went out of him at once. It did not fade; it was shouldered aside, as two men trying to go through a narrow door at once leave one of them on the step, and the Wind's readiness was through the door and the new thing was outside it, gone. He tried it the other way, with the giving face, the faintest stir under his ribs, and it was the same. Whatever this was, it would not stand in the same room as either of the others.

"It took me two years to get it to run while I moved," said Brom. "Two years, and I'd had it since I was fourteen, and nothing else in me to get in its way." He got up off the floor. "You've had it a day and you've got two other things shouting in the next room. You're not going to get it to share by Thursday."

---

They stopped when the sweepers had finished and the noon light was coming grey through the high windows. Brom stood up and gathered his coat off the nail, and Cael stayed where he was on the floor, because his knees and his head between them had decided he would.

He told Brom what he had. It was not much, and he made himself say all of it, the small parts too, because a capability said smaller or larger than it was would be a lie he would end up fighting on.

"Sitting still or standing still. Quiet. One strong one, close, three paces or nearer. A few breaths to open it, if I'm lucky and I don't try. A few to hold it. Then something moves and it's gone, and a minute after, the head." He looked at his hands. "On a floor, against somebody, it'd be worth nothing. With more than one person near me I don't know what it would do. In a room with a crowd in it—" He did not finish. He had a good idea what a crowd would do to a thing that could be knocked over by one big man shifting on a bench.

"Useless in a bout," Brom agreed. He put his coat on. "Mine was useless for a year. At home they had a word for what you do in that year, and I've forgotten it, and it doesn't matter." He did up the top button and stood with his hand on it, looking down. "But that's not the thing I want you to take home tonight. The work you'll do. You work harder than anybody I've met."

Cael looked up.

"Everything else you've got, you point," said Brom. "The step goes where you send it. The thing in your arm goes into whatever you hit. The way you watch, the hooks and the columns, that's pointing too; you aim your whole self at a man and pull the truth out of him. You've learned every one of them by aiming." He shook his head, slowly. "You'll never aim this. Try to turn it into something you aim and all you'll get is a worse pair of eyes than the ones you've already got. And you'll never find out what it was."

"What is it?"

"It's for *knowing*." Brom said it plainly, the way he said everything, and then stood a moment as though he were checking it, and found it would do. "Not for winning anything. Knowing who's in the room. What they are. Where they are, before you've looked." He put his hand on the arch. "I've read your book. You've been finding things out for their own sake since before you came over the hills. You don't need me to tell you that's worth something."

He went out, and Cael heard his steps go across the main floor, and the side door open, and the cold come in, and the door shut.

He sat on the alcove floor a long time with the band round his head and his hands open on his knees, and did not try to feel anything at all.

After a while the sweepers came to the arch with their brooms and looked at him, a boy sitting cross-legged on the stone in an empty alcove with his eyes half shut, and decided among themselves without a word to do that corner last. He heard them go on along the wall. He heard the side door, and somebody calling for the chestnut man, and the first of the noon newcomers coming down the floor from Keth's ring, the tall lad laughing at something, all of it ordinary and far away.

He thought that he had spent a year learning to see everything. He had sat on the east bench with his eyes doing the work of a whole body, and been proud of what they found, and paid for it in aches and a blind spot ten degrees wide. And now there was a thing lying on his skin that did not want him to look at anything at all. It wanted him to be the floor, and let the weight come down on him, and say what it was.

He did not know whether he could do that. He did not know anybody who had ever asked it of him before, except a man in a lit alcove with a garden wall in his past, and an old man at home with his hand on a mill shaft. He found he was smiling, alone, on the cold stone, with his head aching.

---

He wrote it that evening at the crate desk, with the lamp turned low because of his head, and the Power Log open to the new entry. He had begun it the morning before, in ink, with the notice copied at the head; now he gave it the fields, all six, in the order he would never change. He did it slowly, and plainly, and left out nothing he had been given, and nothing he had paid.

*IRON-ADJACENT.*

*What it is: the edge of B.'s read. A thin thing on the skin, all over, outside. It doesn't go out. Things come in.*

*Source: B. Third morning, mid-reset, not asked for. Ruling: same road as the other two. Close, and long, and a real person, and not taken. Found afterward.*

*Conditions: still. Quiet. Not trying. The moment I aim at it, it shuts. Ruling: I have to stop asking before it answers. I'm worst at that of anything.*

*Deployment range: one strong one, close, three paces or under. Opened once in about forty tries, then twice more. Held four breaths, three, five. Lost every time he moved, or I did. Gives: where, which way, that he's alive, a shape I haven't words for. Doesn't give: how far, exactly. Who. Anything moving. Anything at range.*

*Costs: attention itself, whatever I look with. Four breaths cost what a night of the gaze costs. A band round the head a minute after, an hour or more. And it won't share: if the hip leans or the hollow stirs, it goes. Opening it means putting both of the others down.*

*Open questions: Two people in a room. A crowd. Somebody who isn't kindled. Whether it gets cheaper, like his. Whether it ever runs while I move. What it's for, if it isn't for anything I point. B.: it's for knowing.*

He read the entry through: it was the shortest *Deployment range* in the book and the longest *Open questions*. That was right; that was what the book was supposed to look like.

There was one more thing he made himself write, because he could feel himself wanting to fold Tuesday into it. The new thing had come from Brom's read, and on Tuesday Brom's read had lost him, and the two lay side by side in his head as neatly as a lock and a key. That neatness was the reason not to trust it. He turned back to Tuesday's page, to the third exchange, and set down beside it, in pencil, only what he had seen of each.

*Not the same thing. This came with a hush and a notice; the third came with neither. This lies on my skin and I can feel it lying there; the third left nothing on me to feel, before or after. This takes something in, for a few breaths, a man as big as a door; in the third I took nothing in. I was looking at his shoulder. This costs a band round the head; the third cost nothing I can find. This opens only when I'm still and stop aiming; the third came while I was moving, in the middle of an exchange. This is a piece of his read. The third was his read finding nobody there. One I have and can't use yet. The other I never had, and can't find. It stays where it fell.*

Then he turned forward to the new entry again and wrote at the bottom, outside the fields, because there was one more true thing and he wanted it said:

*I own something I cannot use. Twice before I've owned something I couldn't use, and both times the road from owning to using was a winter of mornings like this one, on stone, getting it wrong in front of somebody patient. I know that road now. I know what it costs a day. Begin.*

He capped the ink.

He sat a while with his hand on the closed book. His head had eased to a dull pressure. Through the wall he could hear Lira moving about her room, not yet asleep, not yet gone down the hill to her lamp.

He let it drift open without meaning to. He was only sitting very still, tired, with nothing to aim at, and the thing on his skin found the stillness on its own and opened, a crack. And there through the wall, two paces off, faint, there was somebody: a light quick weight, never still for long, like a bird on a sill that keeps shifting its feet.

He shut it.

He shut it hard, the way you shut a letter you have found open on somebody's table and realise is not yours. The band came back round his head for his trouble. He sat with it and did not mind it.

Then he opened the Log again, to Tuesday's page, to the narrow empty column at the edge headed *Carrying*, and after a while he wrote the first thing in it, very small.

*L., through the wall. Didn't.*

He would show her, since she had asked him to show her first.

Down at the bottom of the hill, through the window over the alcoves, the practice lamp came on, and he watched it for a while.

On Monday Dace would chalk the main season on the wall by the slate, and the floors would fill, and somebody would want him on their card who had not wanted him a week ago. He had three things that were not a Path now, and one of them he could not use in any room with more than one person in it. He had a friend whose whole Path was knowing where people stood, and who still had nowhere to put him. He had a partner at a lamp in the north window who had not yet decided what she thought of that friend, and was entitled to take her time.

He blew out his own lamp, and lay down, and let the cold room be a cold room, and slept.

