editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-30.md:207:*Your letter came on the Thursday, after,* Hesk had written. *I read it at the table and knew from the first line you'd be writing me another, because a lad who expects to win writes about the bread. So I've had two days to make up my mind you lost, and I'm sorry if you didn't, and gladder than I can say about the boy. You found him. I could tell from how you wrote it. My father used to say a man who knows what he's afraid of before and tells you after is worth two who don't. The race has ice at the edges now and the wheel runs slow. Mind the knees. Whatever they are, mind them. H.*
