editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:1:# Chapter 33 — Section Twelve
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:69:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:107:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:123:He looked at the classification block on the first leaf, at its three lines. The registry's, with its stamp. His own, the green slip, with *H.* and a date some weeks gone. And between them the line with nobody's name, in the short printed form the registry used, a designation and a figure, and an empty column at its end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:135:Havel signed the loans book. *Manual, back sections, vols. ii and iii. Quarterly review, river side. H.* The date.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:145:*Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:151:A Priority Level 4 designation, said the manual, was reserved for practitioners of Gold tier or above under active security review. It authorized monitoring at a weight and frequency the manual set out in a table, which Havel read and did not quite believe. It was to be entered only on the written authority of an officer of a grade the manual named, who would sign and date the entry. Every such entry would carry a date for its own review, not more than a year from its making, and would lapse if not renewed in the same hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:153:He read the table after that, because he could not stop himself. A practitioner under Level 4 was to be visited by an assessor not less than once a month and reported on in full each time, on a longer form than any Havel had ever filled in. A named officer two grades above the assessor was to read every report and initial it. Any travel was to be known beforehand, any change of lodging within a day, any new associate within a week. There was a column for the costs, which were large, and a line that said to whom they were to be charged.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:167:This was not dull. This was two things on one leaf that could not both be true by the manual's own words. A Level 4 needed a Gold practitioner and a signature, and the file had neither. And it sat there in its proper place, in its proper form, as quietly as the registry's own stamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:173:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:179:*Contact of record, Ardenmere file. On quarterly review I find a Priority Level 4 designation entered beneath the subject's classification without the officer's signature, date or review date required by Section 12. The subject holds no tier and is under no security review on any record in the file. The designation cannot be correct under the manual as written. Please advise who entered it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:187:*Contact of record, Ardenmere file (Hesk-ward, C.), river side. Quarterly review. A Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4 designation appears in the classification block. I am unable to confirm it against Section 12 of the manual for the review record. Requesting guidance on its origin and continued application. H.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:195:---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:217:*Quarterly review. The line is* Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4. *Manual, Section 12: Gold tier and above, under active security review. Officer's signature, date and review date required. None here. Subject: no tier, no incident. Queried contact of record in the plainest words I had. Answered the same afternoon:* Follow standard procedure. Do not attempt to reclassify the monitoring level. File regular reports. *Complied.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:223:*Who enters a Level 4 and doesn't sign it? And who answers a question about it in three hours without answering it?*

