editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-34.md:7:It was the main floor, the Thursday evening card, the third bout. The lamps were lit but not all of them, sixteen or so, a season Thursday and not a big night; there were perhaps two hundred on the benches. The four girls from the wall were in a row by the water barrel, with the dock partner behind them. Brom was at the rope beside Cael at the north post, with his coat on and his arms folded on the top strand, and he had said nothing since they came in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-34.md:71:A Stone off his heels was a Stone that could be moved. In the fourth Lira moved him twice, both times with the staff in behind his lead knee as he came round, and the second time he went down onto one hand on the stone and the benches came up off their seats. In the fifth he came off his heels a third time, slower now, heavy, and she turned on her left heel outside him and he went past the place she had been. The staff came round off the step into the side of his neck, short and flat, held back at the last as Vell's rule asked. He stood very still for a moment, and then lifted his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-34.md:111:He was a fighter, a big Force from the salt end whom Cael knew by sight. He had fought on the second card and lost, and had sat down on the end of the west bench in the dark to wait for a friend, and had gone to sleep there sitting up with his chin on his chest and his arms folded and his mouth a little open. He was snoring, very faintly. What came off him, this close, without Cael asking it anything, was only a man asleep: slow, low, going nowhere, the way a mill-wheel ticks over at night on the last of the race. Brom at supper had given off something like it, only older and banked. This was not banked; it was only sleep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-34.md:117:*False reading. West wall, end of the card, a weight lying low and still. I read it as somebody hiding. It was a man asleep. The read gave me* somebody, low, still. *I gave it* hiding. *That part was mine.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-34.md:121:*The read reports presence, not intent. The intent is something I add, and I haven't earned the right to add things yet. Check it with my eyes before I believe it with my skin.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-34.md:137:It was not sleep: he knew the sleeping man's slow rise and fall now, and this had none. It was not Brom's banked fire either, low and steady and never thought about. This was kept. Whatever reached him from the back wall reached him at exactly the same weight, breath after breath, not a hair more, not a hair less, as if somebody were minding it. It was the way a fighter stands primed and does not build, for a whole count of ten, because he has decided to. And there was something in it, faint, that he could feel without going looking, the way you can feel a stone under a blanket without lifting the blanket: a hardness kept back. It felt to his skin a little like what he had put his knuckles into on the alcove crosses for three mornings. It was not the same, but it was a cousin to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-34.md:161:"I'm sure there was somebody. The read says what came off them came off the same, every breath, the whole time, like a thing being kept. My eyes say there was a person in a dull coat at the back wall who wasn't watching the bouts." He made himself say the rest. "Last week I was sure about a man who turned out to be asleep. So I'm telling you what I've got, and not what I think it means."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-34.md:177:It was the middle of the morning, and the row was full. They had come up from the baker's to the pie stall at the top, because Lira wanted a pie and said she had earned one. Cael was walking with the thin thing open on his skin, asking it only the one small question, *is there anybody*, and it was saying *yes* and *yes* and *yes* in the way it did in a crowd, so often that it was nearly no use at all. He had only kept it open to practise.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-34.md:179:At the pie stall he stopped, to pay, and when he stopped, the door opened wider on its own, the way it did now when he stood still. And among all the *yes*es, close, four or five paces off across the stall row, there was one that did not come and go like the others. It sat, four or five paces off, and pressed on him at the one unchanging weight he had carried home from the back wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-34.md:183:It was hard, because he was standing, not sitting. The row was full of people going past between him and the thing, each one a *yes* that went across it like a hand passing in front of a lamp. He had to hold it through all of them, and every one cost him. He held it for the time it took the pie woman to find change for a mark, and wrap the pie, and drop the change, and pick it up, and he did not let go. It was the longest he had ever held anything on his feet, a minute, perhaps, a little more, and at the end of it he was sure. Nothing in it had risen or fallen, and the hard thing was still there under the blanket. The same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-34.md:187:When he could look, he looked across the stall row, as if at the paper stall beyond it. There was a plain dull coat at the paper stall with its back to him, its collar up, turning the pages of a broadsheet without reading it. As he looked, it folded the broadsheet and put it back on the pile, and went away down a side lane without once turning round, at an ordinary pace, as anybody might.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-34.md:193:She did not look at the side lane. She took the pie from the woman, and paid for it out of her own pocket because he had forgotten his coin was in his hand, and put the change in his coat. Then they walked back down the row together. Cael noticed that she was walking closer to him than she had walked up it, on his left side, between him and the mouths of the lanes. She did not say anything about it, and neither did he.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-34.md:197:*The market. Same one. Four or five paces, across the stalls, me standing, the row full. Held it about a minute to be sure, from outside: never more, never less, the hard thing under. Cost: the morning and the afternoon. They walked off as I looked, and didn't look back.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-34.md:201:*They can keep that up for a whole card. A minute of them, on my feet, took my whole day. Right now they can wait much longer than I can listen. Watch whether that gets better or worse.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-34.md:205:The third time was a week after the market, and it was close enough that he could not pretend about any of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-34.md:213:It was eight paces off, perhaps less. He did not need a minute this time, or anything but the first breath of it; he knew it as you know a voice in the next room that you have heard twice before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-34.md:257:"That's nothing I know," he said at last. "Not from home. Not from the river city, and I had my read on every kind of Path that city had in it." He frowned at the wall. "Nobody I ever met could keep it that even, on purpose, for a whole card. You don't hold it. You use it or you don't. Either that's a way of doing it I've never come across, or whoever's doing it learned somewhere a long way from anywhere I've been." He looked at Cael. "I don't like either."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-34.md:261:"Can you read them back?" said Brom. "More than *there*. If you sat still long enough, near enough. Could you get anything else?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-35.md:5:He knew the hand: every letter on the front was the same height as its neighbour, level as a ruled line, the hand of a young man who had been told once that neatness was a kind of honesty and had believed it. Coss had read that hand on a routing card some weeks ago, on a short return from the river side, and had gone down to the long room at the end of the day to look at a file he had no reason to look at. He had not expected to see it again so soon. He had not expected to see it at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-35.md:7:He opened it at his desk with the door shut and read it standing. It was four lines long. The third named the designation in full, and the fourth asked, as politely as a thing can be asked, where it had come from.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-35.md:25:A supplementary note was not a query, and asked nobody anything. It went up, into the routing above an officer's own grade, with his name at the foot, and it said a thing he wanted the registry to know he had said. Most men wrote two or three in a career, when a file had gone bad and they wanted it on record that they had seen it go. Coss had never written one, because he had seen what became of men who did, mostly nothing, and he had always preferred nothing that he had chosen to nothing that was done to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-35.md:31:He read it back: it was careful, and it accused nobody. It did not say the line was wrong, or ask who had entered it, or mention the grey slip that had come and gone from the inside of the cover a year ago. It said only that a junior had noticed, and that others would, and that the officer of record had said so, on this date, in his own hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-35.md:39:Then he opened the log itself, not to today's page but back, past a year of ordinary entries, to the two pages he had left empty on purpose. The first was the day of the grey slip, and the second the day he had read Havel's return in the long room. They had stayed empty because a thing written down could be found, and he had thought that the safest thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-35.md:83:He could not have said how he understood it, because nothing had been said to him. But he had worked in the building eleven years, and he knew the difference between a paper that had been lost and a paper that had been read. A lost paper made a small noise somewhere: a clerk asking after it, a routing card come back marked *not found*. This had made no noise at all. Somebody had read his note, and had decided, somewhere above any floor he could see, that it would not be answered. That was the answer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-35.md:99:Whether finding nothing was a comfort, he did not know. He thought about it often, in the late evenings, after his daughter had gone up and the fire was down. Either there was only the one, or there were others and he did not know enough to see them, and he could not tell which. He had learned to look for a thing only by seeing it once, and a man who has seen a thing once does not know what it looks like. He only knows what it looked like that time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-35.md:109:The registry kept, in a set of books on a floor below the long room, an index of every marker on every file in the building. Each had a line of its own, and every line had a field at its end for the marker's provenance: the officer, the order, the floor. Nobody below a certain grade had cause to open those books, and few above it ever did. Against this marker, in that field, the index read, in the same square print as everything else:
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-35.md:119:A junior assessor had read the designation and asked about it, and had been told to stop asking. A senior officer had sent a note upward about it, and had heard nothing back. Neither man had seen the index, and neither knew that the field existed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-35.md:125:They tried to read the watcher back on the next main-floor night, and it was the longest Cael had ever sat still in his life.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-35.md:131:"At the other end," Brom had said. "A bench's length. Like before." He had looked at Cael over his wound hands. "I'll have mine open. Mine's further than yours when I'm sitting, a bit. Between us we might get more than *there*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-35.md:157:Nothing changed. That was the whole of it, for a long time, breath after breath. It did not rise when the last knot of fighters went out past it at the side door, close. It did not fall when the sweepers came in with their brooms. It did not shift when Dace began to wipe his wall, and the chalk dust went up white in the lamplight, and somebody dropped a bucket by the water barrel with a bang that made Cael's door shudder and nearly shut. He held it through the bang, and did not know how.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:5:He had spent a week after the back bench trying to make the read last while Brom moved, because he wanted it to last; he had sat a whole card and a whole sweeping still enough to feel somebody at the far edge of a room. Somewhere under that he had begun to want, very badly, a version of the thing that would be worth something on a floor, against a man who did not stand still to be found. So every morning in the alcove he opened the door sitting, and Brom stood up, and took one slow step, and the door went out. He opened it again, and Brom lifted a hand, and it went out. Brom walked a slow circle round him at three paces and it went out on the first stride, every time, forty times, sixty, while the band came and went and came.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:9:"That's what it does. It's a door. I hold it open."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:37:"It's not what mine does," said Brom, on the third morning. He was watching Cael's face as he said it. "Not really. Mine never knocked. I just learned to let it run while I went. Yours knocks." He thought about it. "It's yours, I think. The knocking. Not mine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:67:"I know you know. That's not why I'm telling you." She put the pen down. "You carried my lamp, that first time, and didn't touch anything. You stood at this table a few weeks ago with your book and read it out in front of forty people, and every line was dated. And you stood on that bench the night of the red lines and watched me do it, and asked me after whether it was worth it." She looked at him. "That's three things, and I don't need four."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:83:"The lamp stays where it is. On the bracket. Not in your hand, not on the shelf, not on the table, not nearer to an open page than the length of my arm." She held out her arm to show him the length. "Heat takes ink faster than years do. My keeper had a book go brown in an evening from a lamp held close, and he never forgave himself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:95:The room was very quiet, the quietest he had been in for weeks, with nobody in it but the two of them and the books, and he felt the thin thing on his skin begin to come open by itself, the way it did now whenever he was still. Vell was a pace from him, and had been Bronze once, and would be a strong clear weight on the front of him if he let it come, and from there it was a short step to going in. He had not asked her. He let it close.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:99:Her own books first, along the wall. She took down one from near the end of the row, from her first years, and opened it on the table, and then one from ten years ago, and one from last season, and laid them side by side, open, square. Three books open at once: he looked at her, and she said, "Mine. I'll be careful with mine as I like." He looked at the three hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:135:Vell read one for him, unfolding the short forms with her finger under them. *Hits from further than his arm.* Another: *Does not move when struck.* Another, beside a woman's name: *Quick. Quicker on the left.* There was no tier, no rank, no name for what anybody was, only what each had been seen to do on a particular night by six or seven people standing at the edge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:147:He read it three times.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:157:He took the grey book out of his coat, with his hands still clean and dry, and opened it on his knee below the table, away from the old page. In the smallest hand he owned, smaller than the tally in the corner, small enough that he would need the lamp to read it back, he copied the first sentence. He left the brackets off, because they were the keeper's and not his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:179:"Kindled at fourteen, Copper the same day. I climbed on my own for four years, in yards, and at eighteen the guild house up the river took me on and put me on its letters. Iron at nineteen. Bronze at twenty-one. They used to bring the younger ones to watch me practise." She said it without any pride in it at all, as she would have read out a result. "I had a year of that. Not even two. Then I made a thing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:195:"The Compact does falsify things," he said. He did not say it as a question.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:215:It was very cold, and very clear, with the stars out hard over the river and the water going by black under the wall. The gulls were asleep in a long row on the coping, heads under their wings, and did not move for him. Down on the water a barge went by with one lantern at its stern, very slowly, and a man on it was singing under his breath. At the corner by the eel market the chestnut man was banking his brazier for the night with a shovel, and he lifted the shovel to Cael without a word. Up in the lanes somewhere a woman was calling a child in by name, twice, and then a door shut. None of it would go in any book. It was only the district, being itself in the dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:223:Everything else was his. The pull toward it was his, and the empty column in Vell's book that it had made him think of. So was the feeling, which he did not trust, of a key lying beside a lock. He had been wrong once already this season about a man asleep on a bench, because he had taken what the read gave him and added a thing of his own to it, and the thing he added had been what he was afraid of. He did not know what he was afraid of here, or what he was hoping, and that was reason enough to add nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:227:He thought about that for a long time, on the river wall, in the cold, because it was not like him any more. He had told Lira about the read through the wall, and about the watcher on the same day she asked. He had told Brom the whole of the Log. He had stood in Vell's back room and said the Compact falsified things out loud. He had spent a season learning that the things you keep shut cost you, and that some of them were about other people, who had a right to know.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:241:It was a week after that, on a night with no card and the house quiet, that he took Keth's pages out and read them through from the beginning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:249:He laid the pages side by side under the lamp and read them as he would have read a fighter he meant to meet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-32.md:33:He could smell it from the corner of the row: onions, and something under the onions that had gone past brown and was arguing about black. When he came in at the front door the hall was blue with it, and the sister was standing at the foot of the stairs with a dish-cloth over her nose and mouth, flapping the street door to and fro to let it out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-32.md:57:He stood in the doorway of Lira's room and looked at it: the narrow bed, the little table with its three odd plates, the staff in the corner and the spare one behind it, the window with the alcove lamp a small far light below. He looked at all of it the way he had looked at Vell's book on his first day, from the front, as if he meant to read it to the end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-32.md:119:Cael had expected him to be. Everything his eyes told him said Brom was resting: the loose shoulders, the cup held in both hands, his legs out under the table, a big man at his ease after three bowls of barley. On the alcove floor, with Brom standing still to be found, the weight had been loud and plain, a man holding his read open on purpose. This was not that; this was quieter. But under the ease, under the cup and the loose shoulders, Brom's read was still going.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-32.md:133:"I can feel it." Brom did not sound angry. He sounded the way he had sounded about the bruise, interested, and a little pleased, and something else. "Not much. Like somebody standing too close behind me in a queue." He drank. "Your range is terrible."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-32.md:187:"Not provisionally." Brom shrugged. "I read people for my living. You can tell me I'm wrong if you like. I won't be any less right."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-32.md:235:They heard the street door, and his steps going away down the row on the frost. Lira stood in the doorway a while longer, listening to them go, and then she came back in, and looked at the room, and began to stack the plates.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-32.md:247:*Eight breaths, seated, an arm and a half, a quiet room, nothing wanted from it. Opened easily. B. at rest is not at rest: his read never shuts. It runs low, under everything, like a banked fire. He felt me at eight. Band after, an hour.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-32.md:267:Lira had chalked a line on the alcove floor, a short straight stroke from the back wall toward the arch, and she was standing on one side of it with her staff laid across her shoulders and her hands hooked over the ends. Brom stood on the other side, in his shirt, with his coat on its nail and his hands unwound, looking down at the line as if it had been written in a language he could almost read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-32.md:281:Cael watched them for most of a quarter-hour from the dark of the arch, and neither of them saw him; or, he thought, Lira had not, and Brom must have felt him come up on the read and had said nothing, because it was not his morning to be watched. Lira was hard on him, harder than she had ever been on Cael in the first winter, and she explained less. She would show it once at the speed she meant and once slowly and then not again, and when he got it wrong she told him exactly how, and when he got it right she said nothing at all and came round him faster.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-32.md:291:"Then read my feet," said Lira, and came round him again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:5:He found it out on the market row, on a grey morning in the second week of the season, with Brom beside him and a loaf under each of their arms. They had come up from the baker's door by the long way, slowly, because Brom had said that if Cael was going to try it on his feet he had better try it somewhere he could not fall down a stair. Cael had been trying it for a week, in the alcove and on the walk home and once, foolishly, on the boarding-house stairs, and every time it had gone the same way: he would open it standing still, and take one step, and it would go out like a candle carried too fast across a room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:7:On the market row it went out three times in the first twenty paces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:13:"You're asking it *who*." Brom shifted his loaf to the other arm. "Every time somebody comes near us, you want to know who it is and where they're going and what they've got in their basket. I can feel you want it. And it can't tell you that while you're moving. Mine couldn't for a year." He nodded down the row at a woman coming up it with a basket of turnips. "Ask it something smaller."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:19:So Cael tried that. He let the door come open, standing still, by the water trough at the corner of the row, and then he walked on, at a slow even pace, the pace he would have used to carry a full bucket, and he asked it nothing at all except that one thing. *Is there anybody.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:21:The woman with the turnips came past on his left, close, a pace off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:29:He got almost to the top of the row before a cart came out of a side lane with its iron tyres banging on the cobbles, and he looked at it, and the door slammed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:39:They had stopped at the corner where the row met the square, to let a carter's dray go by. Cael was standing still, and the door was open, and there were three people near them waiting for the dray too: a girl with a basket of eggs, an old man with a stick, and a young man in a blacksmith's apron with his sleeves rolled up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:65:*Kindled or not,* he wrote, that night, *close, standing still, with leave. Working or resting, close, standing still, with leave. Walking: yes or no only, thirty paces once; that's the street's, and needs nobody's. Nothing else. That's the whole of the vocabulary.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:83:"He made me stand on the line," said Lira. "And he stood there with his eyes shut for about as long as it takes to boil an egg, and I nearly hit him. Then he said it." She drew in her breath. "He said my feet are past it. He said they were past it before I ever came to this city, and he could feel it the first morning, the way my weight goes before I do. He said in my feet I read like an Iron Wind, and better than some."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:89:They had reached the corner of the row, and Lira stopped under the lamp there, and Cael stopped with her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:99:She picked up the staff and they went on up the row. At the door she stopped again, with her hand on the latch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:101:"I thanked him," she said. "He went red. All up the back of his neck, the way he does." The corner of her mouth went up. "Somebody ought to tell him he does that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:111:He had not let himself know that he was waiting, because he was not a man who looked forward to files, but he had kept the date of its turn in the back of his notebook, the one with the cardboard cover, in pencil, small, among other dates that meant nothing. He had watched the rota come round toward it all season, a desk at a time, the way a man watches weather come up a valley. When the supervisor read out the morning's stacks along the long table and put a thin grey folder from the river side on top of a dyer's renewal, Havel felt something in his chest that he did not care to name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:117:He filled in the first five in a quarter of an hour. Residence: the keeper's book, his own visit, nothing since to say otherwise. Contact log: his own entry, and Coss's few, and nothing new. No guild. No incident. No travel outside the district on any record that had come up to the floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:121:He had ticked that box two hundred times without opening the manual, as everybody did. A handling marker was a line in the classification block, entered by somebody with a right to enter it, signed and dated in the right-hand column. To confirm it against the manual meant to read the line and see that it was the kind of line the manual allowed, and every line on every file he had ever drawn had been the kind of line the manual allowed. Every one had initials beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:133:The supervisor was at his desk by the window with the morning's returns, and did not look up when Havel stood beside him and asked for the back sections of the manual against a quarter's review. He only held out his hand for the review sheet, read the sixth box, and gave it back, and then pushed the loans book across the desk with one finger and went back to his returns.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:137:The back sections of the manual lived on the top shelf behind the supervisor's chair, in two thick books bound in the same grey board as the files, with their corners worn white. He had held them once, in his training year, when an instructor had passed them round the table so that each new assessor could feel the weight of everything he would never need to read. Nobody had opened them then. He carried them back to his desk now as carefully as a full tray. He was aware of the man by the door who hummed, and of the two assessors he shared the rota with, and of the fact that none of them looked round. A man carrying the manual to his desk on a review day was a man doing his job.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:141:It took him a long time to find. The short form on the file was a run of four letters and a gap and a figure, and the index ran to forty pages of short forms in close print, by letter, with their long forms beside them and a section number after each. He went down them with his finger as he went down a contact log, a line at a time, and did not let himself skip. Two-thirds of the way through the fourth letter he found it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:143:He read the long form, and read it again, and copied it on the back of the review sheet in his own hand, so that he could not later tell himself he had misread it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:149:Section Twelve was in the third volume, and it was short, three pages, which surprised him; most of the manual ran to great length about small things, and this ran short about something that was plainly not small. He read the whole of it twice before he let himself understand any of it, and then he read the first paragraph a third time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:151:A Priority Level 4 designation, said the manual, was reserved for practitioners of Gold tier or above under active security review. It authorized monitoring at a weight and frequency the manual set out in a table, which Havel read and did not quite believe. It was to be entered only on the written authority of an officer of a grade the manual named, who would sign and date the entry. Every such entry would carry a date for its own review, not more than a year from its making, and would lapse if not renewed in the same hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:153:He read the table after that, because he could not stop himself. A practitioner under Level 4 was to be visited by an assessor not less than once a month and reported on in full each time, on a longer form than any Havel had ever filled in. A named officer two grades above the assessor was to read every report and initial it. Any travel was to be known beforehand, any change of lodging within a day, any new associate within a week. There was a column for the costs, which were large, and a line that said to whom they were to be charged.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:157:*Cael Hesk-ward.* The classification, in the registry's square print: *[SHATTERED]*. No tier. That class carried none; it was the whole of the trouble with it. No guild. No incident anywhere in the record, not so much as a fine. A residence in a boarding house two streets from a warehouse, confirmed by a keeper's book. A boy on a pump step in a market square who had answered every question on the form correctly and then, unasked, had held out a pear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:169:He thought of the four old files in the archive, the only others of that class there had ever been, which his training year had read. Every one of them had carried markers, and every marker had been signed, and dated, and explained, in a hand anybody could find in the duty records. Not one of them had carried this.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:177:The first draft came out of him fast, in a quarter of an hour, and he read it back and was frightened by it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:179:*Contact of record, Ardenmere file. On quarterly review I find a Priority Level 4 designation entered beneath the subject's classification without the officer's signature, date or review date required by Section 12. The subject holds no tier and is under no security review on any record in the file. The designation cannot be correct under the manual as written. Please advise who entered it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:181:Every word in it was true. He could have defended each sentence to the supervisor line by line. And it read, from the first line to the last, like a junior assessor telling a senior officer that something in the senior officer's file was wrong, and asking for a name. He imagined it arriving on a desk two floors up, and the man at that desk reading *cannot be correct*, and *who entered it*, and then reading the initials at the foot. He had stood outside the duty board for four years learning which words made a man known, and *who* was the first of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:187:*Contact of record, Ardenmere file (Hesk-ward, C.), river side. Quarterly review. A Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4 designation appears in the classification block. I am unable to confirm it against Section 12 of the manual for the review record. Requesting guidance on its origin and continued application. H.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:189:He read it back four times. It asked for nothing a contact of record could not give with one line. It did not say *wrong*, and it did not ask *who*. It said only that a box on a printed sheet could not be ticked, which was true, and was the registry's own problem and not his. If anybody two floors up ever wanted to know why a junior assessor had asked about a marker above his grade, the answer was clipped to the inside of the file. The sheet had told him to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:191:He knew, sitting there with it, that it was still a question with his initials on it, going up. He had decided against this once, in the yellow room, on a careful man's reasons. He was not sure the reasons had changed. What had changed was that he had now read Section Twelve, and he could not unread it. A correct man, he had thought then, would raise a line with no name on it, and a careful man would wait until he knew what he was raising. He knew now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:199:It came down in the last round of the afternoon, in an inner-post envelope with his name on the front in a careful upright hand, every stroke of it the same weight. He knew the hand from the contact log, from a year of very short entries. He opened the envelope at his desk while the man by the door hummed, and unfolded the slip inside, and read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:205:He read it twice, and noticed, as a careful man notices such things, that it had come back the same afternoon. Queries to a contact of record waited a week at least, and a fortnight in the season. This one had been answered within three hours of leaving his desk. He did not know whether that meant the question mattered more than he had thought, or that somebody wanted it finished before it could start to matter. He did not know which of those he would rather it meant.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:207:He did what the slip said. He put it inside the file, behind the review sheet, as procedure required for any instruction on a monitoring file. In the sixth box, instead of a tick or a cross, he wrote in his smallest hand, *Queried; instructed per contact of record*, and his initials. That was true, and it was all the box would hold. He closed the file and set it on the pile for the morning's return.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:217:*Quarterly review. The line is* Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4. *Manual, Section 12: Gold tier and above, under active security review. Officer's signature, date and review date required. None here. Subject: no tier, no incident. Queried contact of record in the plainest words I had. Answered the same afternoon:* Follow standard procedure. Do not attempt to reclassify the monitoring level. File regular reports. *Complied.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:219:He read it back. It was true and it said no more than he knew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:233:It burned quickly, the way thin office paper does. He watched the edge go brown and then bright along the lines of his own level hand. He read them going, without meaning to: *cannot be correct*, and *under the manual as written*, and, last, curling up black at the very end of the sheet, *who*. Then that was ash too, and the grate was dark again, and there was nothing in the room but a smell of burnt paper and a young man on his knees in his coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-31.md:35:"I don't say anybody paid anybody." The man had practised this too; Cael could hear it. "I say the odds were false. Everybody in this building knows he does something nobody can see. The Arbiter can't follow it. The registry can't put a name to it. He's got no Path on his card." He lifted the paper a little. "How is a man meant to lay an honest coin against a fighter whose whole ability isn't written down anywhere?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-31.md:67:Vell looked at him for a long moment over her spectacles, and he could not read what was in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-31.md:73:She did not read it at once, but opened her own book first, the day book, and turned back through it to Tuesday, and found the north side floor's afternoon card, where the junior keeper had written and she had written under him. Then she laid the two books side by side on the table, square to its edge, his small hand beside her large square one. The man in the academy coat came a step closer to see, and so did half the near bench.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-31.md:77:So he read it, as he had read the Log to Brom's face in the alcove, a line at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-31.md:89:Cael turned back a page, to the two entries above Tuesday's, and put his own finger under them. "His lead shoulder. Before the rush. It goes down a finger's width and the rim goes with it." He moved the finger to the first line. "I saw it the first time three weeks before. That's the date. I wasn't sure." He moved it to the second. "Eleven days after, against the tannery boy. That's the date. Then I was."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-31.md:105:"Two records," she said. "Kept by two people, in two hands, on the same afternoon, neither knowing what the other wrote. One of them three weeks old before the bout began." She laid her palm flat on her own book. "They say the same thing in the same order. The boy read your Shield's shoulder because the Shield showed it to him twice, in front of you, on my floor. There's nothing in that the registry needs to be told about. It's called watching." She picked up her pen. "The bout stands. The claim's answered. You can take your paper wherever you like."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-31.md:129:"I've had fighters through that door since before you were born," said Dace, "and not one of them could tell me what happened in his own last bout without a quarrel. You read yours out like a carter reading a bill of lading."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-31.md:143:"A book with dates in it," said Dace. "And names. And what each man does with his left shoulder before he goes in, and how long it took the boy who wrote it to find out. Kept the same way every night. Cross-checked against the keeper's." He did not look at the door. Cael saw him not look at it. "Good thing we don't share."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-31.md:149:"I think about it every time Vell takes her book home under her arm." Dace rubbed his chalky thumb on his trousers. "Every fighter on that wall is on it because the registry's ladder either threw him off or never had a rung for him. This floor is the one place in the river towns where what a man can do is written down by people who don't answer upstairs. If a book like yours went up the hill, it wouldn't be a record any more. It'd be a list."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-31.md:157:"And the house had his whole history inside a season. Every bout he'd fought on my floor, who he'd beaten and how, what he'd been paid. Not from my book. I never gave it. From people in the crowd, and the betting slates, and a keeper up the river who didn't know any better." Dace looked at his own hands. "The first time he asked them for anything, a better slot, a bigger share, they read him his own past back to him. Every unsanctioned bout, by date. Just so he'd know how it would sound if anybody upstairs ever wanted to hear it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-31.md:173:He had thought of the book, for a year, as the most private thing he owned, after the Log, and he had never once thought of it as dangerous. He thought now of the Shield's page, and the river-academy man's, and Keth's, with all its small notes about a finger off a grip. He thought of the two entries, dated, that had just ended a man's complaint in front of forty people. The same two lines in the wrong hands would tell a stranger exactly how to beat a Shield from Orvet's gym, and exactly when Cael had learned it, and from where. A record did not care whose hands it was in; it said the same thing to everybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-31.md:185:She read it once, quickly, and then again slowly, and then she put it down flat and laid her hand on it and sat very still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-31.md:191:He read the line upside down. It was a bout of two years ago, on the main floor, on a season night: a Shield, an Iron formal from a house up the river, against a man Cael knew. Everybody in the district knew him: a Shield too, broad and quiet, from the brickworks end, about thirty, who taught Shield work to newcomers in a hired yard for a copper a lesson and was on Dace's wall most weeks in good slots. The line said he had beaten the Iron formal in the second exchange. Under the result, in Vell's hand: *Iron formal guarded left throughout.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-31.md:215:"Wendel hangs off a different nail," said Vell, without looking up. "So do you. So does the boy." She ruled her first red line under the brickworks Shield's name, very straight. "Go and warm up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-31.md:245:He had seen nine of those lines in an old book in his first week, and thought them the cleverest thing he had ever seen in a ledger. He had done the sum then, admiring it: a dozen corrections in thirty years, each one said aloud to the face it cost. He had not watched one happen, or seen a young woman crying at a wall, or a man kick a bench, or the keeper standing still in front of him while he said it. He had not known a record's honesty would have a sound, and that the sound would be somebody's voice going up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-31.md:257:"Go home, Cael," she said. "And next time you think a record of yours is only your own business, remember the girl at the wall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-30.md:13:Cael did not go. His knees would have carried him, though they still had opinions about stairs; but there were forty people between him and the wall, and he could read the wall from where he was, which meant he could read the forty people as well. He watched who went to the top of it first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-30.md:39:Lira read it, and read it again, and did not touch it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-30.md:65:"Then when it does," said Lira, "tell me the same day. Not four days after." She began on the bread at last. "I'd rather know I've been heard than be kept from it. Being kept from things is what Fenmark did." She chewed, and swallowed, and pointed the end of the loaf at him. "And if you ever find out what I sound like when I'm cross, don't tell me that. I don't want to know."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-30.md:83:He began to understand on the fourth morning, and it was from his knees that he learned it. They had been bad on the stairs, and he had come down to the alcove tired and sore and in no mood to try for anything, and had sat down on the stone as a man sits who does not mean to get up for a while. And the door opened at three paces and stayed open. Brom stepped back to four, and it stayed; to five, and Cael did not go after him, because he was too tired to go after anybody. He only sat there, and let the big man move away across the alcove, and the weight on the front of him went with Brom as the warmth of a fire goes when you walk away from it. It thinned, but it did not stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-30.md:91:The band came round his head a minute later, hard, and he sat with his palms over his eyes while it settled.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-30.md:113:And before it, every time, his lead shoulder dropped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-30.md:123:His knees were good, not new but good; two weeks of the stairs and the alcove crosses had brought them back to something he could trust for a short floor and four exchanges if he did not ask them to do anything clever. He had decided, walking down, what he would ask of them, and it was not much. The Wind on a read, or not at all. The Pressure left where it was; the Shield did not hit hard enough to need it answered, and he did not want it on anybody's page. And the new thing on his skin, he would not open at all. On a floor, against a man who was moving, it would be worth exactly nothing, and it would take the other two down with it when it went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-30.md:141:Cael did not look at the club, or the rim, or the man's face. He looked at the lead shoulder, at the place where the shirt met the strap of the shield, and he waited, and he did not breathe more than he needed to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-30.md:145:The hip went on the read. It went as it went now when he had paid for it with watching first, cleanly, cheaply, with the half-breath held. The floor went short, front-left, half a body along the chalk and out of the line, and he was in the lock, two *ands* of bright stillness with the world going by in front of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-30.md:159:Cael went back to his mark and went over himself. The hip line was there, thin, the read price, a pencil line and not a brushstroke. The forearm rang where the rim had caught it, his knees were fine, and his breath came back into its count in four. He had spent one burst and nothing else, and he still had his whole afternoon in him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-30.md:163:He knew, Cael thought. Not what it was. But he knew that something he had done had been read, and that it was his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-30.md:175:Vell came over from her table while the young man was still blotting, which she did not usually do for an afternoon card, and read the line over his shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-30.md:199:At the crate desk he wrote the bout plainly, a line an exchange, as he always did. *First: survey. Rim fast on his left; my left slow; took the rim on the forearm. Second: walked me to the rope. Waited. The drop came. One burst on the read, front-left; lock; ribs. Third: he held the drop up by force, and the rush died without it. Two steps, no burst. Ribs. Hand up.* Under the second exchange he wrote the cost: *one read burst, thin line.* Under the third he wrote the thing he liked best about the afternoon, which was that he had not needed the hip at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-30.md:207:*Your letter came on the Thursday, after,* Hesk had written. *I read it at the table and knew from the first line you'd be writing me another, because a lad who expects to win writes about the bread. So I've had two days to make up my mind you lost, and I'm sorry if you didn't, and gladder than I can say about the boy. You found him. I could tell from how you wrote it. My father used to say a man who knows what he's afraid of before and tells you after is worth two who don't. The race has ice at the edges now and the wheel runs slow. Mind the knees. Whatever they are, mind them. H.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-30.md:209:Cael read it twice. He had not told Hesk about his knees; he had told him nothing at all yet about the bout. He sat a while wondering how a man at a kitchen table four days off by post had known to mind them. Then he understood that Hesk had not known anything; he had only known Cael, and guessed what a boy who lost to a man like that would have paid for it.

